Calc keeps Excel's caption column on XLSX pivots with only column fields

Excel leaves the first column of a pivot table with column fields and no
row field for the data caption: "Sum of Revenue" sits beside the data
row, and the column area starts in the second column. Calc started the
column area in the first column and wrote the caption into the corner,
where the column header then overwrote it. A save therefore moved every
cell one column to the left and wrote firstDataCol="0" (Ely R15.11i).

Pivot tables from Excel files (they carry a column header caption, see
0071) now keep that column, with the caption on the data row, unless the
data layout field is itself a column field. The export follows from the
output range. Calc's own pivot tables keep Calc's layout.

A pivot table with no value field has no caption, so it keeps no such
column; otherwise the table grew into the cell beside it. The caption
is written as text: a value field named "=1+1", "'Revenue" or "123"
stays that text, as in Excel, and is not read as input.

